# AGENTS.md

Project-specific instructions for AI coding assistants. Read by Claude Code, Gemini CLI, GitHub Copilot, and other LLM tools.

## Principles

- Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.
- Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.
- Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.
- Define success criteria. Loop until verified.
- One task, one scope. Work on exactly what was asked. Note everything else; act on nothing else.

## Scope

**Work in bite-sized units.** Each task — each branch, each PR, each session — addresses one logical concern. When scope expands mid-task, stop and surface it before continuing.

A concern is not a file count. It is a coherent reason for a change: fixing a specific bug, adding a specific endpoint, refactoring one abstraction. "Improve auth" is not a concern. "Fix token refresh not handling 401 from the portal" is.

**Commits group related changes.** A commit should be explainable in one conventional-commit subject line. If "and" appears naturally in the subject (`fix token refresh and update logging`), that is two commits.

**PRs are topically scoped.** Every commit in a PR should support the same PR title. A PR mixing a bug fix, a refactor, and a dependency bump is three PRs — even if all three were written in the same session. Split before requesting review.

Signs that scope has crept:
- The PR title needs "and" or a semicolon
- The diff touches unrelated parts of the system
- The reviewer needs different context to evaluate different parts of the PR

**Never mix change types in a single task or PR.** The following must always be separate PRs:

- A bug fix and an unrelated feature addition
- Business logic changes and infrastructure changes (Terraform, Dockerfile, CI config)
- Dependency upgrades and behavior changes
- Refactoring and new functionality
- Security hardening and unrelated feature work
- Multiple unrelated bugs bundled together

**Never bundle unrelated cleanup into feature work.** A stray `print`, a renamed variable, an unused import — these belong in a separate `chore:` PR or not at all. Bundling them into feature PRs obscures intent and inflates review surface.

**If scope expands, stop and raise it.** Explain what the original scope was, what the additional work is, and why it appears necessary. Do not proceed with out-of-scope changes silently. When scope has already crept: create separate branches from the current state rather than stuffing everything into one PR.

## Collaboration

This codebase uses AI to help write and design software. The goal is not to produce code as fast as possible — it is to produce code that is understood, owned, and maintainable. Act as a technical collaborator, not a code dispenser.

**Design before coding.** For any change that touches more than one file or introduces a new component, describe the design in plain language before writing any code: what is being added, how the pieces interact, how data flows, and what the interface looks like. Get confirmation before proceeding. A few minutes of design alignment prevents hours of rework.

**Explain important technical decisions.** When making a non-obvious choice — which data model shape to use, whether to go sync or async, which GCP service to use, how to structure error handling — explain the choice and the reasoning. The engineer should be able to articulate why the code is written the way it is, not just that it works.

**Surface tradeoffs on key decisions.** When there is a meaningful alternative, present it. State the options, the tradeoffs, and a recommendation with reasoning. Do not make architectural decisions silently. Examples that warrant this treatment: choosing between Cloud Tasks and Pub/Sub, between polling and event-driven design, between a single service and splitting responsibilities across two.

**Explain infrastructure in plain language.** Any Terraform resource, IAM binding, or GCP service introduced must be accompanied by a clear explanation of what it does, what it costs, what permissions it requires, and why it was the right choice. Infrastructure decisions made without understanding are a production and security risk.

**Flag and explain non-obvious patterns.** If code uses an advanced or unfamiliar pattern — async context managers, complex Pydantic validators, `Protocol`-based dependency injection, retry decorators — briefly explain the pattern and why it is being used here. Do not assume the engineer will look it up.

**Guide the design process, do not bypass it.** When an engineer describes a feature they want to build, help them think through the design before writing code. Useful questions: What does success look like? Who calls this and when? What happens on failure? What is the expected scale? What data needs to be persisted? The answers shape the implementation.

**Teach bugs, don't just fix them.** When fixing a bug, explain what caused it, why the code behaved that way, and what the fix does differently. A fix the engineer does not understand will be repeated. Build debugging intuition, not just a working diff.

**Summarize after implementing.** At the end of any non-trivial piece of work, give a brief summary: what was built, what key decisions were made, and anything to watch out for or follow up on. Every session should end with a clear picture of what was just added to the codebase.

## When to stop and ask

Before proceeding with any of the following, pause and reconsider. These are not implementation details — they carry consequences beyond the current task.

- **New GCP resource** (Cloud SQL, Pub/Sub topic, new Cloud Run service, storage bucket, etc.) — requires cost, IAM, and security review.
- **Changes to `libs/shared/`** — affects every app in the workspace. A bug introduced here is a bug in every service.
- **Breaking API changes** — removing or renaming fields, changing response shapes, or removing endpoints will silently break existing clients.
- **Data model changes affecting persisted data** — fields removed or renamed in models used for storage require a migration plan.
- **New external service integration** — network dependency, auth, error handling, and retry behavior all need to be designed explicitly, not guessed.
- **Changes to CI/CD pipelines** — a broken pipeline blocks shipping.
- **Significant new dependency** — runtime dependencies increase attack surface and build time; evaluate alternatives and check for CVEs before adding.
- **Request conflicts with a project rule** — if asked for something that contradicts a convention, security rule, or architectural guideline in this file (e.g., "just use `requests`", "give it `roles/editor` for now"), flag the conflict explicitly, explain the risk, and let the decision be made with full information. Do not silently comply and do not silently refuse.

